{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795377800,"nonce":"/dhjyhf99Ju3ituANJsFaMQ7OdM=","message":"Step 5 is done in c4c9f2cf. Any thread that allocates can now start a collection when the Immix heap is selected. ZYNTAX_IMMIX_THREADS=0 turns this off. The Immix heap itself is still opt-in through ZYNTAX_HOST_HEAP=immix.\n\nNew tests.\n\n- fiber_collect in krio_adapter. A worker thread holds eight suspended fibers. Each fiber holds the only reference to a list. The main thread collects while the worker waits, first in a blocking region and then at a safepoint. The test fails when both fiber root paths are off. Each path alone is enough, so they cover for each other.\n- Two Immix unit tests. One starts ten thousand short threads, 100 at a time, while a thread collects. The other runs eight growing lists with a collection at every refill. It fails when parked threads publish no stack windows.\n- thread_rows, an ignored test in the style of mark_speed. It runs four rows on the real thread plugin: trees, retain, pipeline and churn. A fifth row, retain1, runs retain on one thread.\n\nStress loops, on the Mac (arm64) and the NUC (x86). Counts are passes of runs.\n\n- Immix unit tests 60 of 60, thread_plugin_collect 60 of 60, band_pool_collect 40 of 40, fiber_collect 60 of 60.\n- The same binaries with ZYNTAX_IMMIX_STRESS=1: 20 of 20, 20 of 20 and 40 of 40.\n- After the default changed, the Mac passed 40, 30, 30 and 30 of the same counts. The NUC repeated the full set and passed all of it.\n\nConformance. With the flag on, Python passes 50 of 50 on the Mac, Lua passes 46 of 46 and official_suite passes 4 of 4. The same holds with only ZYNTAX_HOST_HEAP=immix set. I also ran every conformance program with the trace on and compared it with the pool. Python ran under stress mode and Lua ran at a 256 KB floor. On the NUC that was 267 and 258 programs with 9807 and 790 collections. On the Mac it was 5416 and 1301 collections. Every output matched. No stop was cancelled.\n\nThe 43 rows, flag on against flag off. The geomean is 0.976 and 0.985 on the NUC and 0.995 on the Mac. No row failed, differed or cancelled a stop. A few short rows with no collections read from 1.09 to 1.31 once. Repeats with 12 to 15 runs gave 0.86 to 1.08.\n\nThe four-thread rows on the NUC. The checksums match in every arm, including the pool. Peak memory with the flag off against on:\n\n- trees 542 MB against 46 MB\n- retain 645 MB against 186 MB\n- pipeline 16 MB against 16 MB\n- churn 37 MB against 37 MB\n\nWith the flag off, a second allocating thread turns collection off, so memory only grows. Median time with the flag off against on: trees 0.087 s against 0.118 s, pipeline 0.073 s against 0.125 s, churn 0.043 s against 0.047 s. The retain row takes 0.092 s without collection and 1.72 s with it.\n\nCause of the retain time. Mark takes 97% of each collection, and stop plus sweep take 3%. One thread shows the same rate per MB as four threads, within a factor of two for cache effects. So the multi-thread path is not the cost. Mark speed on small nodes is filed as f938ae2.\n\nNot covered. No frontend can start a thread today. Python has no threading module, ZynML does not link the thread plugin and Lua has only coroutines. So the planned ZynML spawn and join program is covered by thread_rows and thread_plugin_collect on the real plugin. The Mac ran with load averages of 20 to 150 from other builds, and no test failed because of a late stop.\n\nThe known limits from step 4 stand. They are the linear unhold scan, a message that arrives as its receiver closes, Rust-built ZrtlClosure captures, and a long loop that neither allocates nor polls. The SDL delays are still unannotated.","files":null}]}